   IINNTTEERRFFAACCEE AADDDDRREESSSSEESS
     The mmiibbIIII module handles a table of interface IP-addresses.  These ad‐
     dresses are held in a

           struct mibifa {
                   TAILQ_ENTRY(mibifa) link;
                   struct in_addr  inaddr;
                   struct in_addr  inmask;
                   struct in_addr  inbcast;
                   struct asn_oid  index;
                   u_int           ifindex;
                   u_int           flags;
           };

     The (ordered) list of IP-addresses on a given interface can be traversed
     by calling mmiibb__ffiirrsstt__iiffiiffaa() and mmiibb__nneexxtt__iiffiiffaa().  The list should not
     be considered read-only.

   IINNTTEERRFFAACCEE RREECCEEIIVVEE AADDDDRREESSSSEESS
     The internet MIB-2 contains a table of interface receive addresses.
     These addresses are handled in:

           struct mibrcvaddr {
                   TAILQ_ENTRY(mibrcvaddr) link;
                   struct asn_oid  index;
                   u_int           ifindex;
                   u_char          addr[ASN_MAXOIDLEN];
                   size_t          addrlen;
                   u_int           flags;
           };
           enum {
                   MIBRCVADDR_VOLATILE     = 0x00000001,
                   MIBRCVADDR_BCAST        = 0x00000002,
                   MIBRCVADDR_HW           = 0x00000004,
           };

     Note, that the assignment of MIBRCVADDR_BCAST is based on a list of known
     interface types.  The flags should be handled by modules implementing in‐
     terface type specific MIBs.

     A receive address can be created with mmiibb__rrccvvaaddddrr__ccrreeaattee() and deleted
     with mmiibb__rrccvvaaddddrr__ddeelleettee().  This needs to be done only for addresses that
     are not automatically handled by the system.

     A receive address can be found with mmiibb__ffiinndd__rrccvvaaddddrr().

   IINNTTEERRFFAACCEE SSTTAACCKK TTAABBLLEE
     The mmiibbIIII module maintains also the interface stack table.  Because for
     complex stacks, there is no system supported generic way of getting this
     information, interface type specific modules need to help setting up
     stack entries.  The mmiibbIIII module handles only the top and bottom entries.

     A table entry is created with mmiibb__iiffssttaacckk__ccrreeaattee() and deleted with
     mmiibb__iiffssttaacckk__ddeelleettee().  Both functions need the pointers to the inter‐
     faces.  Entries are automatically deleted if any of the interfaces of the
     entry is destroyed.  The functions handle both the stack table and the
     reverse stack table.

